.Dd October 15, 2026
.Dt ZFS-REWRITE 8
.Os
.
.Sh NAME
.Nm zfs-rewrite
.Nd rewrite files, directories, volumes and file systems without modification
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm zfs
.Cm rewrite
.Oo Fl ACPSWrvx Ns Oc
.Op Fl l Ar length
.Op Fl o Ar offset
.Ar file Ns | Ns Ar directory Ns | Ns Ar volume Ns | Ns Ar filesystem Ns …
.
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Rewrite blocks of specified
.Ar file
as is without modification at a new location and possibly with new properties,
as if they were atomically read and written back.
A
.Ar volume ,
given by its dataset name or the path of its device, is rewritten the same way,
and so is a
.Ar filesystem ,
given by its dataset name, with all its objects.
.No See Sx NOTES .
for more information about property changes that may be applied during rewrite.
.Bl -tag -width "-r"
.It Fl A
Rewrite only the blocks that have a copy on a top-level vdev whose
.Sy allocating
property is
.Sy off ,
see
.Xr vdevprops 7 ,
so that they move to vdevs that allocate.
The indirect blocks that have a copy on such a vdev are rewritten too, even
when none of the blocks under them is.
With
.Fl W ,
these blocks are rewritten whatever their size.
.No See Sx NOTES .
.It Fl C
Skip blocks that are shared via block cloning (BRT).
Cloned blocks are referenced by multiple files or datasets.
Rewriting these blocks would create separate copies and increase space usage.
This flag prevents such expansion by skipping cloned blocks.
.It Fl P
Perform physical rewrite, preserving logical birth time of blocks.
By default, rewrite updates logical birth times, making blocks appear
as modified in snapshots and incremental send streams.
Physical rewrite preserves logical birth times, avoiding unnecessary
inclusion in incremental streams.
Physical rewrite requires the
.Sy physical_rewrite
feature to be enabled on the pool.
It is refused for an encrypted volume or file system given by name: a raw
incremental stream would not include the rewritten blocks, and the received
copy could not be read.
.It Fl S
Skip blocks that are shared with snapshots.
Blocks created before the most recent snapshot are shared with that snapshot.
Rewriting these blocks would create new copies, leaving the old copies for
the snapshot and increasing space usage.
This flag prevents such expansion by skipping snapshot-shared blocks.
.It Fl W
Skip blocks whose rewrite would not change their allocated size.
After a RAID-Z expansion, only the blocks written before it completed that the
new width stores in less space are rewritten, so an interrupted rewrite can be
run again without rewriting the same blocks twice.
Blocks smaller than the old width keep their size and stay in place, which can
leave the space freed around them fragmented.
A size change that a property change would cause, as with
.Sy compression ,
is not taken into account.
On vdevs other than RAID-Z, only gang blocks and blocks on removed devices
are rewritten.
.It Fl l Ar length
Rewrite at most this number of bytes.
A file system given by name can not be rewritten in part.
.It Fl o Ar offset
Start at this offset in bytes.
A file system given by name can not be rewritten in part.
.It Fl r
Recurse into directories, and rewrite the directories themselves,
the given ones included.
This has no effect on a volume or a file system given by name.
.No See Sx NOTES .
.It Fl v
Print names of all successfully rewritten files, directories and volumes.
For a file system given by name, print the progress after each step, and once
it is done, what was rewritten and what was left alone.
.It Fl x
Don't cross file system mount points when recursing.
This has no effect on a volume or a file system given by name.
.El
.Sh NOTES
Rewrite works by replacing an existing block with a new block of the same
logical size.
Changed dataset properties that operate on the data or metadata without
changing the logical size will be applied.
These include
.Sy checksum ,
.Sy compression ,
.Sy dedup
and
.Sy copies .
Changes to properties that affect the size of a logical block, like
.Sy recordsize ,
will have no effect.
.Pp
Rewrite of cloned blocks and blocks that are part of any snapshots,
same as some property changes may increase pool space usage.
Use the
.Fl C
and
.Fl S
flags to skip cloned and snapshot-shared blocks respectively to prevent
this expansion.
Holes that were never written or were previously zero-compressed are
not rewritten and will remain holes even if compression is disabled.
.Pp
On a RAID-Z vdev, rewritten blocks use the width of the vdev once its
expansion has completed, see
.Xr zpool-attach 8 .
Blocks rewritten while the expansion is in progress keep their old width.
Blocks that are already deduplicated stay where they are, and keep their width.
.Pp
If a
.Fl l
or
.Fl o
value request a rewrite to regions past the end of the file or volume, then
those regions are silently ignored, and no error is reported.
.Pp
By default, rewritten blocks update their logical birth time,
meaning they will be included in incremental
.Nm zfs Cm send
streams as modified data.
When the
.Fl P
flag is used, rewritten blocks preserve their logical birth time, since
there are no user data changes.
.Pp
A rewrite of the whole of a file, which is the default when neither
.Fl l
nor
.Fl o
is given, also rewrites its spill block, which holds the system attributes that
do not fit in its dnode, such as extended attributes stored with
.Sy xattr Ns = Ns Sy sa ,
and the block that holds its dnode, with the other dnodes in that block.
A rewrite of the whole of a volume also rewrites the object that holds its
properties, the blocks that hold its dnodes, and the block of its objset.
.Pp
With
.Fl r ,
each directory reached is rewritten as a whole: the blocks that hold its
entries, and as for a file, its spill block and the block that holds its dnode.
The changes to it that add or free blocks, and all changes to a small
directory, wait while a part of these blocks is read, a part of at most 1 MiB
at a time.
Directories are not rewritten when
.Fl l
or
.Fl o
is given, which only apply to files.
Rewriting a directory needs the permission to add entries to it, since it can
not be opened for writing.
.Pp
The
.Fl A
and
.Fl S
flags apply to the spill blocks, the blocks that hold dnodes, and the blocks of
directories, as to the others.
The
.Fl P
flag does not: as they are metadata, they are rewritten with a new birth time,
and incremental
.Nm zfs Cm send
streams then include the dnodes in the rewritten dnode blocks, and the
rewritten spill blocks and blocks of directories, but no file data.
.Pp
A volume is given by its dataset name, such as
.Ar pool/volume ,
or by the path
.Pa /dev/zvol/pool/volume
of its device.
A name that is also an existing path is taken as a path.
Unlike for a file, which only needs write access, rewriting a volume needs the
same privilege as other pool configuration operations.
The volume can be in use, and does not need to have a device node:
it is rewritten in pieces, so that I/O to it waits for one piece at most.
Until the command is done, the volume can not be destroyed, rolled back,
received into, shrunk, or sent itself rather than a snapshot of it, and its
pool can not be exported.
Unlike for a file, the command does not return before the rewritten data are
on stable storage.
A volume snapshot, a read-only volume, a volume received from a redacted send
stream, or a pool that is not writable cannot be rewritten, nor can a volume
whose intent log has not been replayed yet, which happens when its device is
created.
If the device of the volume is removed meanwhile, for example because its
.Sy volmode
property is changed, the command may stop with an error, and can then be run
again.
The volume block size is not changed.
.Pp
The
.Fl A
flag helps to empty a top-level vdev before it is removed with
.Nm zpool Cm remove :
once its
.Sy allocating
property is set to
.Sy off ,
rewriting the files and volumes with
.Fl A
moves their blocks off it, data, indirect and spill blocks, and the blocks that
hold their dnodes, and with
.Fl r
the blocks of the directories, without reading or writing the blocks that are
elsewhere already.
A gang block is always rewritten, since the vdevs of its parts are not looked
at.
A vdev that is being removed does not count, since the removal copies its data
anyway.
The blocks that other flags skip stay where they are, and so do the old copies
that snapshots, or other files that a block was cloned to, still refer to,
until these are destroyed or rewritten as well.
Only the blocks of the files, directories and volumes themselves are
rewritten: other metadata, such as the objects that a file system keeps for
itself, or the extended attributes stored in hidden directories with
.Sy xattr Ns = Ns Sy dir ,
may remain on the vdev, unless the file system is rewritten by its name.
The metadata of the pool itself is rewritten by
.Nm zpool Cm rewrite ,
which also rewrites every file system and volume of the pool by name, see
.Xr zpool-rewrite 8 .
.Pp
A file system is given by its dataset name, such as
.Ar pool/filesystem ,
and as for a volume, a name that is also an existing path is taken as a path.
It is rewritten whole: all its objects, the files and directories that no path
reaches included, such as those hidden under a mount point or in hidden
extended attribute directories, with their spill blocks and the blocks of
their dnodes, and the objects that the file system keeps for itself, such as
its master node, its delete queue, its system attribute tables, its FUID
table and its quotas; then every block that holds dnodes, the user, group and
project accounting objects, and the block of the objset.
Its child file systems are not included: each is rewritten by its own name.
Rewriting a file system needs the same privilege as rewriting a volume.
.Pp
A file system is rewritten in steps of about 64 MiB or a second each, which
the command repeats until it is done; interrupting it stops it within a step,
and running it again starts over, which with
.Fl A
skips the blocks that were moved already.
A mounted file system can be in use: its files and directories are rewritten
under the same locks as by path, but the quotas of their owners do not stop
the rewrite.
One that is not mounted is held exclusively for each step, during which an
attempt to mount, roll back, receive into or destroy it fails as busy.
The command does not return before the rewritten blocks are on stable storage.
A snapshot, a read-only file system, one being received or received from a
redacted send stream, one whose intent log has not been replayed yet, which
mounting it does, and one that is encrypted while its key is not loaded cannot
be rewritten, nor can a file system of a pool that is not writable.
.Pp
Some blocks of a file system rewritten by name are left alone, and reported
with
.Fl v :
the blocks that
.Fl S
and
.Fl C
skip, counted as blocks; the files in its delete queue, which are freed once
they are no longer open, counted as objects; and while it is mounted, the
objects that are neither files, directories nor objects of its own, such as
the access control lists that file systems of an old version keep apart from
their files, also counted as objects.
Its intent log blocks are not rewritten: they are freed once the changes they
log are written.
The snapshots of a file system keep their blocks where they are, as the
snapshots of a volume do.
.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr vdevprops 7 ,
.Xr zfsprops 7 ,
.Xr zpool-features 7 ,
.Xr zpool-attach 8 ,
.Xr zpool-remove 8 ,
.Xr zpool-rewrite 8
